VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Post-termination transition period (PTTP) definition
(3, 'S Corporations', 'What is the post-termination transition period (PTTP) for an S corporation?', '["A one-year period after S termination during which AAA distributions remain tax-free and suspended losses may be deducted against stock basis", "A six-month period after S termination during which the corporation continues to file Form 1120-S", "A three-year waiting period before the corporation may re-elect S status", "A two-year period during which the corporation pays both entity-level and pass-through taxes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1377(b), the PTTP generally runs for one year after the last day of the final S corporation tax year. During this period, distributions from AAA are tax-free returns of previously taxed income, and shareholders may deduct suspended losses to the extent of stock basis. Choice C incorrectly describes the unrelated five-year re-election waiting period under Section 1362(g).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — LLC electing S corporation status
(3, 'S Corporations', 'What must a domestic LLC do before electing S corporation status under Subchapter S?', '["Convert to a statutory corporation by filing articles of incorporation with the state", "File Form 8832 to elect corporate classification, then file Form 2553 for S status", "Obtain a private letter ruling from the IRS approving the S election", "Amend the operating agreement to limit membership to 100 members"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 1361(a), only an entity classified as a corporation may elect S status. A domestic LLC defaults to disregarded entity or partnership classification. By filing Form 8832 (Entity Classification Election), the LLC elects corporate classification, after which Form 2553 may be filed. Choice A is incorrect because the check-the-box regulations allow federal reclassification without changing the state entity form.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Distribution of appreciated property
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Redwood Corp, an S corporation, distributes equipment with a fair market value of $80,000 and an adjusted basis of $50,000 to its sole shareholder, Jennings. What are the tax consequences of this distribution?', '["Redwood recognizes no gain, and Jennings takes a $50,000 carryover basis in the equipment", "Redwood recognizes a $30,000 gain taxed at the entity level at 21%, and Jennings takes a $50,000 basis", "Redwood recognizes a $30,000 gain that passes through to Jennings, and Jennings takes a $50,000 basis", "Redwood recognizes a $30,000 gain that passes through to Jennings, and Jennings takes an $80,000 basis in the equipment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 311(b), an S corporation distributing appreciated property recognizes gain as if sold at FMV. The $30,000 gain ($80,000 FMV minus $50,000 basis) passes through to Jennings via Schedule K-1. Jennings takes an $80,000 FMV basis in the equipment. Choice A is incorrect because, unlike partnerships under Section 731, S corporations must recognize gain on appreciated property distributions.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — LIFO recapture tax on C-to-S conversion
(3, 'S Corporations', 'What is the LIFO recapture tax that applies when a C corporation using LIFO inventory converts to S corporation status?', '["A tax on the excess of FIFO value over LIFO value of inventory, included in the final C corporation return", "A tax on unrealized appreciation in LIFO inventory layers measured at fair market value", "A tax on all inventory at the lower of cost or market on the conversion date", "A special excise tax of 10% on the LIFO reserve balance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1363(d), a C corporation using LIFO that elects S status must include the LIFO recapture amount — the excess of FIFO inventory value over LIFO value — in income on its final C corporation return. The tax is paid over four equal installments beginning with the last C year return. Choice B is incorrect because recapture is measured as the FIFO-to-LIFO spread, not unrealized appreciation to fair market value.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Closing of the books election
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Ironwood Corp, a calendar-year S corporation with two equal shareholders, Fletcher and Garrett, operates all year. On June 30, 2026, Fletcher sells her entire 50% interest to Harmon. From January 1 through June 30, Ironwood earned $240,000 of ordinary income, and from July 1 through December 31, it earned $60,000. All shareholders consent to a closing-of-the-books election under Section 1377(a)(2). What are the income allocations?', '["Fletcher: $120,000; Garrett: $150,000; Harmon: $30,000", "Fletcher: $75,000; Garrett: $150,000; Harmon: $75,000", "Fletcher: $150,000; Garrett: $150,000; Harmon: $0", "Fletcher: $120,000; Garrett: $120,000; Harmon: $60,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1377(a)(2), with all affected shareholders'' consent, the S corporation closes its books on the transfer date. Fletcher receives 50% of Period 1 income: 50% x $240,000 = $120,000. Garrett receives 50% of both periods: $120,000 + $30,000 = $150,000. Harmon receives 50% of Period 2: 50% x $60,000 = $30,000. Choice B incorrectly applies the default per-share, per-day method, which would prorate $300,000 evenly regardless of when income was earned.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Qualified Subchapter S Subsidiary (QSub)
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Timberline Corp, an S corporation, owns 100% of the stock of Cascade Subsidiary Inc. Timberline elects to treat Cascade as a qualified Subchapter S subsidiary (QSub). What is the federal tax effect of this election?', '["Cascade files its own Form 1120-S and issues separate K-1s to Timberline", "Cascade retains its separate entity status but shares Timberline''s single S election", "Cascade is disregarded as a separate entity, and its assets and liabilities are treated as belonging to Timberline", "Cascade is treated as a separate C corporation subject to entity-level tax"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1361(b)(3), a QSub election causes the subsidiary to be disregarded as a separate entity for federal tax purposes. All of Cascade''s assets, liabilities, income, and deductions are treated as those of the parent S corporation, Timberline. Cascade does not file a separate return. Choice B is incorrect because a QSub does not maintain separate entity status — it is fully absorbed into the parent for federal purposes.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Section 444 fiscal year election
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Oakmont Corp, an S corporation, wants to use a September 30 fiscal year-end instead of the required calendar year. The corporation files a Section 444 election. Under this election, what annual obligation does Oakmont incur to offset the tax deferral benefit?', '["Oakmont must make a required payment under Section 7519 approximating the tax deferral benefit", "Oakmont must obtain annual shareholder consent for the non-calendar year", "Oakmont must file a separate reconciliation schedule with each shareholder''s K-1", "Oakmont must pay a 5% penalty tax on its pass-through income for each month of deferral"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 444, an S corporation may elect a fiscal year resulting in no more than a three-month deferral. To compensate the Treasury, Section 7519 requires an annual required payment calculated to approximate the tax on deferred income. This payment functions as a refundable deposit. Choice D is incorrect because there is no penalty tax — the required payment is a calculated deposit based on a statutory formula, not a per-month penalty.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Estimated tax payments for entity-level taxes
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Summit Industries Inc., a former C corporation that elected S status two years ago, has both accumulated earnings and profits and significant built-in gain assets. For which entity-level taxes must Summit make estimated tax payments?', '["Only the built-in gains tax under Section 1374", "Only the excess net passive income tax under Section 1375", "Both the built-in gains tax under Section 1374 and the excess net passive income tax under Section 1375", "S corporations are never required to make estimated tax payments"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 6655, an S corporation subject to the built-in gains tax (Section 1374) or the excess net passive income tax (Section 1375) must make estimated tax payments on those entity-level taxes, following the same rules as C corporations. Summit has accumulated E&P and built-in gain assets, making both taxes potentially applicable. Choice D is incorrect because these two special entity-level taxes are exceptions that require estimated payments.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Buy-sell agreement and one-class-of-stock
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Briarwood Corp, an S corporation, has two shareholders: Keller (60%) and Lang (40%). Their buy-sell agreement provides that upon a shareholder''s death, the corporation will redeem the stock at a price equal to three times the most recent year''s net income. The IRS challenges this agreement as creating a second class of stock. Does this agreement violate the one-class-of-stock requirement?', '["Yes, because it creates differing redemption rights among shareholders", "Yes, because the pricing formula does not reflect fair market value", "No, because bona fide buy-sell agreements are disregarded unless a principal purpose is to circumvent the one-class requirement", "No, because redemption agreements are always excluded from the stock classification analysis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1361-1(l)(2)(iii), bona fide buy-sell agreements, redemption agreements, and transfer restrictions are disregarded in determining whether a second class of stock exists, unless a principal purpose is to circumvent the one-class-of-stock requirement. An agreement using a formula rather than FMV is not automatically disqualifying. Choice A is incorrect because differing redemption rights in a bona fide agreement do not create a second class of stock absent a circumvention purpose.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — COD income pass-through with shareholder insolvency
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Ridgeline Corp, an S corporation, has $100,000 of cancellation of debt (COD) income in 2026. The sole shareholder, Porter, has stock basis of $120,000. Porter is personally insolvent by $60,000 (liabilities exceed assets by $60,000). How is the COD income treated?', '["$100,000 is excluded at the entity level and never reaches Porter''s K-1", "$60,000 is excluded under the insolvency exception and $40,000 is taxable to Porter at the entity level", "$100,000 passes through and is fully taxable because insolvency is determined at the entity level", "$100,000 passes through to Porter, who applies the insolvency exclusion to exclude $60,000 and reports $40,000 as income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 108(d)(7)(A), the insolvency and bankruptcy exclusions for COD income are applied at the shareholder level for S corporations, not at the entity level. The full $100,000 passes through on Schedule K-1. Porter then applies Section 108(a)(1)(B) to exclude $60,000 (the extent of insolvency) and reports $40,000 as taxable income. Choice C is incorrect because insolvency is determined at the individual shareholder level, not the corporate level.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Charitable contribution AGI limits at shareholder level
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Crestwood Corp, an S corporation, makes a $50,000 cash charitable contribution during 2026. The sole shareholder, Mitchell, has adjusted gross income of $70,000 including S corporation pass-through income. How is the charitable contribution treated on Mitchell''s individual return?', '["Mitchell deducts $50,000 as an above-the-line deduction with no AGI limitation", "Mitchell deducts $42,000 (60% of AGI) and carries forward the remaining $8,000 for five years", "Mitchell deducts $50,000 because S corporation charitable contributions bypass AGI limitations", "Mitchell cannot deduct the contribution because only C corporations claim entity-level charitable deductions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 170(b)(1)(G), cash contributions to public charities by individuals are limited to 60% of AGI. The S corporation''s charitable contribution passes through as a separately stated item on Schedule K-1. Mitchell applies the individual AGI limitation: 60% x $70,000 = $42,000 deductible currently, with $8,000 carrying forward for up to five years. Choice C is incorrect because pass-through charitable contributions are subject to the same individual-level AGI limits as personal donations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Net investment income tax on non-participating shareholders
(3, 'S Corporations', 'What additional tax may apply to S corporation income received by a shareholder who does not materially participate in the business?', '["Self-employment tax of 15.3%", "Alternative minimum tax at a 26% rate", "Excess business loss surtax of 10%", "Net investment income tax of 3.8%"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1411, a 3.8% net investment income tax (NIIT) applies to S corporation income received by shareholders who do not materially participate, to the extent their modified AGI exceeds the statutory threshold ($200,000 single / $250,000 MFJ). Choice A is incorrect because S corporation pass-through income is not subject to self-employment tax regardless of participation level — only reasonable compensation (wages) is subject to FICA.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Section 1245 recapture vs. Section 1231 gain
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Pinehurst Corp, an S corporation, sells machinery used in its business for $120,000. The machinery had an original cost of $110,000, accumulated depreciation of $50,000, and an adjusted basis of $60,000. How should Pinehurst report the $60,000 gain on the shareholders'' Schedule K-1?', '["All $60,000 as ordinary business income in the nonseparately stated total", "All $60,000 as a long-term capital gain, separately stated", "All $60,000 as a Section 1231 gain, separately stated", "$50,000 of Section 1245 ordinary income and $10,000 of Section 1231 gain, both separately stated"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1245, depreciation recapture of $50,000 (the lesser of $60,000 gain and $50,000 accumulated depreciation) is treated as ordinary income. The remaining $10,000 is Section 1231 gain. Both flow through as separately stated items on Schedule K-1 because they affect each shareholder''s return differently. Choice C is incorrect because it ignores mandatory Section 1245 depreciation recapture, which must be recognized as ordinary income before any Section 1231 gain.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Fringe benefits for >2% shareholders
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Silverleaf Corp, an S corporation, provides $6,000 in employer-paid parking benefits to Delaney, a shareholder-employee who owns 80% of the stock. How is this fringe benefit treated for Delaney?', '["Included in Delaney''s W-2 wages because shareholders owning more than 2% are treated as partners for fringe benefit purposes", "Tax-free qualified transportation fringe under Section 132(f), same as any employee", "Deductible by Silverleaf but not includible in Delaney''s income", "Excluded from income but subject to self-employment tax"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1372, an S corporation shareholder owning more than 2% of the stock is treated as a partner for fringe benefit purposes. Qualified transportation fringes under Section 132(f), including employer-paid parking, are employee-only benefits that do not extend to partners or >2% S corp shareholders. The value must be included in Delaney''s W-2 compensation. Choice B is incorrect because Section 1372 overrides the general fringe benefit exclusion for >2% shareholders.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: hard / L3 — Death of shareholder — step-up in basis and AAA
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Rodriguez, the sole shareholder of Canyon Corp (an S corporation), dies on August 15, 2026. At death, Rodriguez''s stock basis is $45,000 and the FMV of the stock is $200,000. Canyon Corp''s AAA is $120,000. Rodriguez''s estate inherits the stock. What is the estate''s stock basis and the effect on Canyon''s AAA?', '["$200,000 basis with step-up; AAA resets to $0", "$45,000 carryover basis; AAA remains at $120,000", "$200,000 basis with step-up; AAA reduces by the $155,000 step-up amount", "$200,000 basis with step-up; AAA remains at $120,000 unchanged"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1014(a), the estate receives a stepped-up basis equal to the FMV at death ($200,000). However, AAA is a corporate-level account under Section 1368 and is not affected by a shareholder''s death or basis step-up. The AAA of $120,000 continues unchanged and determines tax treatment of future distributions. Choice A is incorrect because AAA does not reset — it persists as a corporate-level attribute regardless of ownership changes among shareholders.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — New shareholder consent not required
(3, 'S Corporations', 'When an existing S corporation admits a new shareholder through a stock purchase, what consent does the new shareholder need to provide for the S election?', '["The new shareholder must sign an amended Form 2553 within 60 days", "The new shareholder must consent annually on the corporation''s tax return", "All existing and new shareholders must re-sign Form 2553 within 75 days", "No consent is required because the S election carries forward to all new shareholders automatically"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 1362(a), once a valid S election is in effect, it continues for all subsequent shareholders without requiring additional consent. Only the initial election requires all shareholders to consent. A new shareholder who acquires stock in an existing S corporation is bound by the existing election. Choice A is incorrect because no amended Form 2553 is required — the election is a permanent corporate attribute, not a per-shareholder filing.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Other Adjustments Account (OAA) definition
(3, 'S Corporations', 'What is the purpose of the Other Adjustments Account (OAA) in an S corporation?', '["Tracking tax-exempt income and related expenses to determine the tax-free portion of distributions", "Tracking cumulative ordinary business income that has been taxed to shareholders", "Tracking accumulated earnings and profits from C corporation years", "Tracking nondeductible expenses such as penalties and fines"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1368(e)(1), the OAA tracks tax-exempt income and expenses related to tax-exempt income. When an S corporation with accumulated E&P makes distributions, the OAA determines the portion attributable to tax-exempt income, which is distributed tax-free and reduces shareholder basis. Choice B is incorrect because cumulative taxed S corporation income is tracked by the Accumulated Adjustments Account (AAA), not the OAA.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Bona fide debt requirement for shareholder loans
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Warner, the sole shareholder of Broadleaf Corp (an S corporation), signs a corporate promissory note payable to Warner for $50,000. However, no cash is transferred, no interest is charged, and no repayment schedule is established. If Broadleaf reports a loss, can Warner use the $50,000 note as debt basis?', '["Yes, any promissory note from the corporation to a shareholder creates debt basis", "Yes, but only if the loss does not exceed $25,000", "No, because the note lacks the economic substance of a bona fide debt and does not create debt basis", "No, because only bank loans guaranteed by the shareholder create debt basis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1366(d)(1)(B) and case law, a shareholder must have an actual economic outlay to create debt basis. A promissory note without cash transfer, interest terms, or a repayment schedule lacks the hallmarks of bona fide indebtedness. The IRS requires the shareholder to have personally made funds available to the corporation. Choice D is incorrect because guaranteed bank loans never create S corporation debt basis — only direct shareholder loans with genuine economic substance qualify.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — PTTP distribution from AAA after S termination
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Westbrook Corp''s S election terminates on December 31, 2025. As of that date, its AAA balance is $70,000 and it has no accumulated C corporation E&P. On March 1, 2026, during the PTTP, Westbrook distributes $90,000 cash to its sole shareholder, Mason, who has stock basis of $85,000. How is the distribution treated?', '["The entire $90,000 is a taxable dividend because S status has terminated", "$85,000 is a tax-free return of basis and $5,000 is capital gain", "$70,000 is tax-free from AAA, $15,000 is a tax-free return of basis, and $5,000 is capital gain", "$70,000 is tax-free from AAA and $20,000 is a taxable dividend"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1371(e)(1), during the PTTP, cash distributions are applied against the AAA balance and are nontaxable to the extent of AAA ($70,000 reduces basis to $15,000). The next $15,000 reduces remaining stock basis to zero (tax-free return of capital). The final $5,000 exceeds basis and is capital gain. Choice A is incorrect because PTTP rules preserve S corporation distribution treatment for cash distributed from AAA.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — BIG tax offset by recognized built-in loss
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Timberstone Corp elected S status on January 1, 2024, with net unrealized built-in gain (NUBIG) of $500,000. In Year 2, Timberstone sold Asset A for a $200,000 built-in gain and Asset B for a $50,000 built-in loss. What is the net recognized built-in gain subject to the Section 1374 tax for Year 2?', '["$150,000", "$200,000", "$250,000", "$500,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1374(d)(2), the net recognized built-in gain for any tax year is computed by netting all recognized built-in gains and losses during that year. Timberstone''s $200,000 built-in gain is offset by the $50,000 built-in loss, yielding net recognized built-in gain of $150,000 subject to the 21% BIG tax. Choice B is incorrect because it ignores the built-in loss offset — Section 1374 uses net recognized built-in gain, not gross gain alone.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Tax year of inclusion for shareholders
(3, 'S Corporations', 'In what tax year does an S corporation shareholder report income or loss from the S corporation?', '["The tax year in which the S corporation''s tax year ends", "The tax year in which the income is actually distributed to the shareholder", "The calendar year in which the S corporation earns the income, regardless of its year-end", "The tax year following the S corporation''s tax year-end"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1366(a), S corporation items are included in the shareholder''s income for the shareholder''s tax year in which the S corporation''s tax year ends. Since most S corporations use a calendar year, the items typically align. Income is taxable whether or not it is actually distributed. Choice B is incorrect because S corporation pass-through income is taxable when the S corporation''s tax year closes, not when distributions are received.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Stock redemption treated as exchange
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Waverly Corp, an S corporation with accumulated C corporation E&P, redeems 100% of Shareholder Barrett''s stock for $80,000. Barrett''s stock basis is $30,000. Barrett is unrelated to the remaining shareholders. How is this redemption treated under Section 302?', '["As a distribution subject to the AAA/E&P ordering rules", "As a complete termination of interest treated as an exchange, with Barrett recognizing $50,000 capital gain", "As a tax-free return of capital up to $30,000 with $50,000 as ordinary income", "As an $80,000 taxable dividend because the corporation has accumulated E&P"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 302(b)(3), a redemption that completely terminates a shareholder''s interest qualifies for exchange treatment. Barrett recognizes capital gain of $50,000 ($80,000 proceeds minus $30,000 stock basis). Because Barrett has no remaining interest and is unrelated to other shareholders, the complete termination test is met. Choice A is incorrect because Section 302 exchange treatment overrides the AAA/E&P distribution ordering when the redemption qualifies as a complete termination.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Distributions from accumulated E&P taxed as qualified dividends
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Clearwater Corp, an S corporation with accumulated C corporation E&P, distributes $40,000 to its sole shareholder, Banks. The AAA is $0 and Banks''s stock basis is $50,000. How is the $40,000 distribution treated for federal income tax purposes?', '["Tax-free return of stock basis", "Long-term capital gain taxed at preferential rates", "Taxable dividend from accumulated E&P, eligible for the qualified dividend rate", "Ordinary income taxed at the shareholder''s marginal rate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1368(c)(2), when AAA is exhausted, distributions from an S corporation with accumulated C corporation E&P are treated as dividends to the extent of that E&P. Under Section 1(h)(11), dividends from domestic corporations qualify for the preferential qualified dividend rate (0%, 15%, or 20%). Choice A is incorrect because return of basis only applies after both AAA and accumulated E&P are fully distributed — E&P takes priority over remaining basis.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Multi-layer distribution with E&P-first election
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Harborview Corp, an S corporation, has AAA of $60,000, accumulated C corporation E&P of $90,000, and OAA of $10,000. Its sole shareholder, Cortez, has stock basis of $140,000. Harborview distributes $170,000 and makes an election under Section 1368(e)(3) to distribute accumulated E&P before AAA. Evaluate the distribution treatment and Cortez''s ending stock basis.', '["$60,000 tax-free from AAA, $90,000 dividend, $10,000 tax-free from OAA, $10,000 capital gain; ending basis $0", "$170,000 dividend because the E&P-first election causes all distributions to be treated as dividends", "$90,000 dividend, $60,000 tax-free from AAA, $10,000 tax-free from OAA, $10,000 return of basis; ending basis $60,000", "$90,000 dividend, $60,000 tax-free from AAA, $20,000 return of basis; ending basis $60,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 1368(e)(3), an S corporation may elect to distribute accumulated E&P before AAA. With the election: $90,000 is a taxable dividend (exhausting E&P, no basis reduction), $60,000 from AAA reduces basis to $80,000, $10,000 from OAA reduces basis to $70,000, and $10,000 return of basis reduces basis to $60,000. This election eliminates E&P to avoid the Section 1375 excess net passive income tax. Choice B is incorrect because only the E&P layer is a dividend.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Basis-suspended loss permanently lost on stock sale
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Andrews holds stock in Lakeview Corp, an S corporation, with $0 stock basis and $0 debt basis. Andrews has a $25,000 suspended loss from a prior year due to the basis limitation. Andrews sells all of her Lakeview stock for $35,000 in 2026. What happens to the $25,000 suspended loss?', '["The $25,000 suspended loss is deductible in 2026 because the sale restores basis", "The $25,000 suspended loss is permanently lost upon the sale of the stock", "The $25,000 suspended loss offsets the gain, reducing the capital gain on the stock sale", "The $25,000 suspended loss transfers to the buyer of the stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 1366(d)(2), losses suspended due to insufficient stock and debt basis carry forward only for the shareholder who incurred them and become deductible only when basis increases. Upon a complete disposition, the shareholder no longer has basis in the S corporation, and the $25,000 suspended loss is permanently disallowed. Choice C is incorrect because the suspended loss cannot offset the capital gain — the gain on the stock sale and the suspended operating loss are separate items requiring basis to be deductible.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L2 — Nondeductible expenses reduce shareholder basis
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Marigold Corp, an S corporation, pays a $3,000 fine for an environmental violation in 2026. How does this nondeductible expense affect the sole shareholder''s stock basis?', '["No effect on stock basis because the expense is nondeductible", "Reduces the sole shareholder''s stock basis by $3,000", "Increases stock basis by $3,000 as a nondeductible expense add-back", "Reduces AAA by $3,000 but does not affect stock basis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 1367(a)(2)(D), nondeductible, noncapital expenses of an S corporation reduce shareholder stock basis. Although the $3,000 fine is not deductible on the corporate return, it still decreases basis because it represents an economic outflow of corporate resources. Choice A is incorrect because nondeductibility for income tax purposes does not prevent the basis reduction — both AAA and stock basis decrease for nondeductible expenses.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Built-in gains tax with C corporation NOL offset
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Ironforge Corp elected S status on January 1, 2023, with NUBIG of $400,000 and a C corporation net operating loss carryforward of $80,000 at the conversion date. In Year 3, Ironforge sells a built-in gain asset recognizing a $250,000 built-in gain with no other built-in items. What is the built-in gains tax liability for Year 3?', '["$52,500 (21% x $250,000)", "$35,700 (21% x $170,000)", "$84,000 (21% x $400,000)", "$67,200 (21% x $320,000)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 1374(b)(2), the net recognized built-in gain may be reduced by C corporation net operating loss carryforwards available at the conversion date. Ironforge''s $250,000 built-in gain is reduced by the $80,000 NOL, yielding a taxable built-in gain of $170,000. The BIG tax is 21% x $170,000 = $35,700. Choice A is incorrect because it ignores the NOL offset — Section 1374(b)(2) explicitly allows pre-conversion NOL carryforwards to reduce the BIG tax base.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Encumbered property contribution basis
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Thornton contributes equipment with a fair market value of $100,000, an adjusted basis of $70,000, and a $40,000 liability assumed by the corporation to newly formed Cypress Corp, an S corporation, in exchange for all the stock in a Section 351 exchange. What is Thornton''s stock basis in Cypress?', '["$70,000", "$30,000", "$60,000", "$100,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 358(a), the shareholder''s basis in stock received in a Section 351 exchange equals the adjusted basis of property transferred ($70,000) decreased by the liability assumed ($40,000), resulting in stock basis of $30,000. FMV is irrelevant to the basis calculation. Choice A is incorrect because it ignores the debt relief — under Section 358(d), a liability assumed by the corporation is treated as boot, which reduces the shareholder''s stock basis.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Passive loss release on complete disposition
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Hoffman, a passive investor, owns 100% of Ridgemont Corp, an S corporation, and has $40,000 of suspended passive losses accumulated over three years. In 2026, Hoffman sells all of her Ridgemont stock to an unrelated buyer in a fully taxable transaction. What happens to the $40,000 suspended passive losses?', '["The losses are permanently disallowed upon sale of the stock", "The losses transfer to the buyer of the stock", "The losses are fully deductible against any income in the year of the complete taxable disposition", "The losses carry forward to offset only future passive income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(g)(1), when a taxpayer makes a fully taxable disposition of an entire interest in a passive activity, all suspended passive losses are allowed in full in the year of disposition, offsetting any type of income. Hoffman''s $40,000 becomes fully deductible on her 2026 return. Choice A is incorrect because the complete disposition rule specifically releases suspended passive losses — unlike basis-suspended losses, which do not release upon stock disposition.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Accumulated earnings tax does not apply to S corporations
(3, 'S Corporations', 'Which C corporation penalty tax does NOT apply to S corporations?', '["Built-in gains tax", "Excess net passive income tax", "Employment tax on shareholder wages", "Accumulated earnings tax"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 531, the accumulated earnings tax penalizes C corporations that accumulate earnings beyond reasonable business needs to avoid shareholder-level dividend taxation. This tax does not apply to S corporations because S corporation income is already taxed at the shareholder level through pass-through treatment, eliminating the tax avoidance concern. Choice A is incorrect because the built-in gains tax (Section 1374) does apply to S corporations that converted from C corporation status.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
